@@ -11,3 +11,11 @@ against a JSON value. The empty string selects the entire document; slash-prefix
 pointers select object members or array elements. Within a token, `~1` represents
 `/` and `~0` represents `~`. The function throws for malformed pointers and
 references that do not exist.
+
+## JSON Patch
+
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` applies an [RFC 6902 JSON Patch](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6902)
+array and returns the resulting JSON value without changing the input document. It supports
+`add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy` and `test`; malformed operations and operations
+that cannot be applied throw an error. Operation paths and `from` values use RFC 6901 JSON
+Pointers.
